      "title": "Policy support, exporter resilience drive strong Oct trade performance: CII",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/economy/news/policy-support-exporter-resilience-drive-strong-oct-trade-performance-cii-126111500334_1.html",
      "description": "The government's support and the strong efforts of exporters helped India's exports reach a record high of nearly USD 50 billion in October, CII's Sanjay Budhia said on Sunday.\nIndia's goods exports increased 45 per cent to USD 49.47 billion in October, compared to USD 34.11 billion in the same month last year.\nImports also rose 16 per cent to USD 89.35 billion from USD 76.69 billion. As a result, trade deficit widened to a year high of USD 39.88 billion.\nBudhia, Chair - CII National Committee on Exports and Patton International's MD, said strong shipments of petroleum, electronics, engineering products and gems and jewellery pushed the country's shipments last month.\n\"India's export performance for October is indeed encouraging and energising. It reflects the resilience, competitiveness and growing global footprint of Indian industry,\" he said.\nThe growth in non-petroleum exports is encouraging, with exports of electronic goods, engineering products, chemicals, gems and jewellery",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-11-15T10:00:16"
    },
    {
      "title": "India, Egypt look to strengthen trade, investment ties: Piyush Goyal",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/external-affairs-defence-security/news/india-egypt-look-to-strengthen-trade-investment-ties-piyush-goyal-126111500135_1.html",
      "description": "India is looking to expand trade and investment opportunities in Egypt, widen market access for its goods and services, and encourage imports of Egyptian products and capital inflows into India to foster mutually beneficial economic cooperation, Commerce and Industry Minister Piyush Goyal said.\nAddressing a community event organised by the Indian Community Association in Egypt (ICAE) here, he said the bilateral trade between the countries stood at about USD 6.5 billion.\nBut for the size of the two economies, \"I believe it's just a beginning; it cannot be our destination; the sky's the limit\", he said here late last evening.\nIndia's exports to Egypt during 2025-26 amounted to USD 3.87 billion, and imports stood at USD 2.60 billion.\nIndia's major exports to Egypt include meat and poultry products, petroleum products, organic chemicals, cotton and machinery, and its major imports include crude petroleum, fertilisers, cotton and mineral products.\n\"We are looking at investment ...",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-11-15T05:33:24"
    }
